  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nOur approach leverages a combination of convex relaxation and coordinate-wise optimization to address the non-convex nature of the overlap integral. We represent h as a piecewise-linear function and iteratively refine its coefficients to minimize the maximum overlap. The key idea is to break the problem into smaller subproblems that can be solved efficiently while ensuring feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Decision variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n_points, with h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n- Objective: minimize max\u2096 (h \u2217 (1\u2212h))(k) * dx\n- Constraints:\n  1. \u2211\u2090 h_a * dx = 1\n  2. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- Each update uses an approximation of the gradient of the maximum overlap with respect to h_i, computed via finite differences.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n- If initial_h_values exist, project them onto [0,1] and rescale to meet the integral constraint. Otherwise, initialize h using a symmetric sawtooth pattern.\n\nb. Optimization loop:\n1. Compute current overlap for all lags and find the maximum.\n2. For each h_i, compute the gradient contribution using finite differences.\n3. Update h using an adaptive step size based on gradient magnitude and feasibility.\n4. Clip h to [0,1] and normalize to ensure the integral constraint.\n\nc. Early termination:\n- Stop when target C\u2085 is achieved or time budget is exhausted.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Initial step size: \u03b7 = 0.003\n- Adaptivity: reduce \u03b7 by 10% every 200 iterations, or when improvements stagnate\n- Perturbations: apply every 100 iterations with magnitude 0.01\n- Number of points: n_points = 70\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each iteration takes ~0.05s, allowing ~12000 iterations in 600s\n- Robust against infeasible updates via projection\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure h satisfies constraints and recompute C\u2085 for verification\n- Check for finite values and proper calculation of overlaps",
